1. where aura comes from
------------------------

aura is a modified version of:

  kek's WiZ Light Controller 1.0
  Copyright (C) 2026 Eshaan Pisal (@kek353)
  https://github.com/kek353/philipswizlightcontroller
  license: gnu gpl version 3

that program is where these come from: local control over the wiz protocol,
light discovery, lights saved by mac address, the color and white modes,
presets, and syncing with the light's real state.

how much of its code is left: aura was rewritten file by file. a line-by-line
comparison of the two code bases finds 10 identical lines out of roughly
2,800 in aura (the color temperature to rgb formula and a few one-liners).
because aura started as a modification of that program and keeps its
behavior, it is a derived work and stays under the same license, gpl-3.0.

changes made in aura (october 2026):
  - new interface written from scratch on qt (pyside6) instead of
    customtkinter: monochrome, dot-matrix headlines, lowercase text.
  - interface in english and spanish.
  - power button with a brightness ring, sound feedback, tray icon, global
    keyboard shortcuts and the --toggle / --on / --off commands.
  - routines: sleep timer, wake-up light, white that follows the time of
    day, and following the pc (lock and sleep).
  - scenes made by the person, wiz built-in scenes, start with windows.
  - music and screen effects.
  - portable mode: settings are saved next to the program.
  - discovery on every network adapter and automatic reconnection when the
    light's ip address changes.


2. ideas taken from wiz-hack
----------------------------

  wiz-hack, by Shravan Revanna (@myselfshravan)
  https://github.com/myselfshravan/wiz-hack
  license: mit, according to its readme

the music and screen effects are inspired by it: frequency bands mapped to
rgb, pulse, strobe, and ambilight-style edge analysis. the ideas were
reimplemented for aura; its code was not copied (the same comparison finds 2
identical lines, both generic).


3. the wiz protocol
-------------------

the local wiz protocol (json over udp, port 38899) is documented by the
community in pywizlight: https://github.com/sbidy/pywizlight


